WEBVTT

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:11.550
 The idea of planting fruit trees in public space up until like two or three years ago was just like, no way, that's not happening.

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:21.630
 And basically the arguments were, you know, a child could get their eyes poked out, they could climb and fall, they're going to throw that fruit at somebody, there's somebody that's going to slip on the fruit.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:27.950
 There's all these reasons why people say that fruit can't be in the parkways in cities or in public parks.

00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:40.140
 So, when we planted a public fruit park in Delaware Park with the LA County Arts Commission, it was a year long of meetings, like trying to defend fruit trees.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:54.080
 The moment when we realized that fruit is unique on the planet, that everyone can relate to it, that the smallest child and their grandparents are both the experts of the taste of what a strawberry is.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:01:10.850
 We realized that fruit and fruit trees and public space could actually be a material that we could apply all of these skills of art making from, you know, I don't know, Austin and I our whole lives, since we were teenagers, we were always artists.

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:29.060
 So, we were able to take things that we learned in photography or other skills and start, I don't know how to put it exactly, but where instead of fruit being the subject of something or public space being the site, we started understanding, oh my God, this could be like clay and collage and papier-mâché.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:31.880
 And that allowed us to really play with the public.

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:36.290
 Instead of the public, again, being an audience, we could think of it as being a collaboration.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:44.680
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:45.680
 I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:51.800
 This season, we're talking with visionary chefs, gardeners, farmers, organizers, artists, and scientists.

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:55.090
 These people have shaped the food movement in California.

00:01:55.670 --> 00:02:05.720
 We talk with a diverse group of California's rebel food makers about the ways they do things in their farms, kitchens, and communities that reshape the way we think about food.

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:08.580
 This show is made by Devin Sampson and Chelsea Wills.

00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:15.160
 Special thanks to the support from Cal Humanities, Food First, and Rebecca Murillo for making this season possible.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:22.660
 Fallen Fruit is an art collaboration conceived in 2004 by David Berms, Matthias Wegener, and Austin Young.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:28.660
 Fallen Fruit began by mapping fruit trees growing on or over public property in Los Angeles.

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:38.140
 The collaboration has expanded to include serialized public projects and site-specific installations and happenings in various cities around the world.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:49.810
 By always working with fruit as a material or media, the projects reimagine public interactions with the margins of urban space, systems of community, and narrative real-time experience.

00:02:50.090 --> 00:03:03.290
 Fallen Fruit's visual work includes an ongoing series of narrative photographs, wallpapers, everyday objects, and video works that explore the social and political implications of our relationship to fruit and the world around us.

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:11.920
 Their current project, The Endless Orchard, is a massive collaborative effort to plant fruit trees in shared spaces around the world.

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:16.820
 Hi guys, welcome to the show. So nice to have you both here today.

00:03:17.080 --> 00:03:18.540
 Hey, it's great to be here.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:20.390
 Hi, good to hear from you.

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:24.970
 David, sounds like you're in your car.

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:28.370
 Well, that's perfect. You're in L.A. and you're in a car.

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:31.230
 No, it's the way it goes, but I do nicer clothes on a bike.

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:31.970
 Yeah.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:46.060
 But, you know, when you're in L.A., it's really strange, depending on where you are, because I would talk to somebody who was walking in L.A., and people actually stopped him and asked him if he was okay.

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:47.610
 What? Really?

00:03:48.110 --> 00:03:57.530
 Yeah, it's just like, you know, he's in a suit, like, walking to, like, a talk or something, and then, you know, people stopped and said, are you okay? What are you doing?

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:03.530
 And he's like, well, I'm going to an audition, so I had to wear this.

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:09.180
 Someone's filming me walking, actually. What do you guys think?

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:20.290
 I think that walking is, like, a newer thing in L.A. You know, it's becoming more of a walking city, but it wasn't ever before.

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:24.240
 I feel like we could talk about L.A. for a whole hour.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:30.540
 No, but that's really how our project started, too, was thinking about that. Yeah.

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:35.630
 Well, why don't you tell me? So your project started, Fallen Fruit started what year?

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:59.580
 Fallen Fruit started in 2004, and it was in response to a call for submissions or projects for a publication called the Journal of Aesthetics and Protest, and the basic idea of the call, kind of paraphrase it, was, is it possible to use the agency of activism without the idea of opposition?

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:08.410
 Like, is it possible to make something that isn't against anything? And that was the kind of spark that started Fallen Fruit.

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:23.670
 I think it was something that maybe we didn't even understand what a great project starting, using that as a starting philosophy would create.

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:33.290
 But at the time, there were three of us. There were three founders for Fallen Fruit, Matthias Wigner and David Burns and myself, Austin Young.

00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:45.450
 And so we thought about, we were thinking about our neighborhood and how there were all these fruit trees growing around, and we thought we'd map them.

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:53.290
 And we decided to only map the ones that were accessible by the public or maybe on public space or hanging over public space.

00:05:53.650 --> 00:05:55.430
 That was really the beginning of the project.

00:05:55.430 --> 00:06:14.130
 And then we also thought about it as a way to, hey, you know, get out of your car, people, you know, instead of driving three blocks to get a lemon, which people do all the time, you know, just walk down the street and pick a lemon and maybe say hi to a friend.

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:20.750
 So we thought about it as a way to, that could bring people together, you know, like we don't even know our neighbors.

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:24.570
 So let's, so let's, let's actually go out and talk to them while we're picking fruit.

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:29.960
 And it kind of like, as soon as we came up with the idea, we were really excited.

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:36.480
 And so we thought, oh, we're going to, we're going to map every city in the world and we're going to, you know, we'll make a website.

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:42.210
 And it just seemed like such an amazing idea where we thought we'll just take this farther.

00:06:42.530 --> 00:06:44.770
 So we kept on going with the project.

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:51.690
 So I kind of imagine like the three of you walking around for some reason, it's at night.

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:57.700
 It's a warm night in LA and you're like, keep finding fruit trees.

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:00.720
 And then you're just snacking and eating and walking.

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:02.060
 Is that what happened?

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:05.240
 Like, what was the first time you went and picked fruit altogether?

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:08.640
 Well, we, we didn't really go out and make a picnic.

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:10.460
 That's not what happened.

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:18.370
 We were thinking about the neighborhood that we lived in, which is Silver Lake and things that were common between us.

00:07:18.370 --> 00:07:26.310
 And sometimes we would call each other or text each other and find out if, hey, are there still any lemons down on that particular tree?

00:07:26.510 --> 00:07:29.210
 Or are the loquats ripe over there yet?

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:30.990
 Like, we would do that anyway.

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:38.050
 So for the first map, I mean, we didn't really know how to make a map that was a walkable distance.

00:07:38.290 --> 00:07:40.350
 We figured it out by making it.

00:07:41.050 --> 00:07:47.970
 And what we learned for the Silver Lake map was time there was over 100 fruit trees in five city blocks.

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:49.520
 It was shocking to us.

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:52.660
 We were totally surprised that the neighborhood was so abundant.

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:57.300
 Also, to put it in more context as well, like, you know, this is before YouTube.

00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:06.680
 This is before, like, this was, we actually came to this project before Vanity Fair released their green magazine.

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:15.200
 It was, like, it was just on the edge of the green movement or thinking about sustainability or thinking about fruit in public space.

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:21.080
 So everyone who sort of we talked to about the project, they got so excited about it.

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:26.260
 And then people would want to come and join us on and look for fruit.

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:36.140
 So we started doing these nocturnal fruit forages, which is funny that you saw us in the dark because we would invite people and tons of people would come.

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:42.400
 And we would go around and, you know, pick bananas and avocados.

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:47.460
 And people would get so excited, they would get nervous to pick a fruit.

00:08:47.930 --> 00:08:49.470
 And then they'd laugh.

00:08:49.730 --> 00:08:50.550
 It was really fun.

00:08:50.550 --> 00:09:01.210
 Well, the reason I ask is because I have all these maps in my head of places that I've lived and all the trees that are there, right?

00:09:01.370 --> 00:09:08.680
 So where I currently live, there's, like, this nectarine tree that's going off right now and the peach tree on the PG&E property.

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:15.070
 And, you know, like, I have my whole route over the course of a whole year.

00:09:15.550 --> 00:09:15.890
 Amazing.

00:09:15.890 --> 00:09:24.330
 And it's funny because it's a map that's, like, made by myself, but it's also made in conjunction with all my neighbors, right?

00:09:24.430 --> 00:09:30.350
 Where, like, some of those trees, I'm like, well, I can pick a couple of peaches if the neighbor's not home.

00:09:30.530 --> 00:09:32.210
 Some of them are in public spaces.

00:09:32.910 --> 00:09:37.210
 You know, but it's, like, something that I can't make by myself, actually.

00:09:37.450 --> 00:09:41.130
 So I was curious, kind of, like, what's your process like that?

00:09:41.470 --> 00:09:48.660
 Like, what is public space and where do those, how do you define what that is in terms of where those fruit trees hang?

00:09:48.940 --> 00:09:50.620
 There's some controversy, I think.

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:54.760
 Yeah, but, you know, conceptually, public space is complicated.

00:09:55.580 --> 00:09:59.390
 And in actuality, it's less complicated than interpretive.

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:02.670
 In concept, it's actually vague.

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:04.850
 It's a gray area in the law.

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:07.840
 There isn't a legal definition of public space.

00:10:09.490 --> 00:10:14.180
 The way that we interpret it and perceive it, there's no national law.

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:16.080
 There's no state law about this.

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:20.860
 There are city laws about what a sidewalk can be used for and things like that.

00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:22.600
 But it's all different in every city.

00:10:22.870 --> 00:10:27.590
 What's more interesting about it, really, conceptually, is that it's common law or common sense.

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:33.930
 And there are legal definitions about what private property is, for example, things behind a fence.

00:10:34.190 --> 00:10:37.030
 But there isn't a lot of definitions about public space.

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:39.610
 I'm sure Austin has some, too.

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:41.430
 That's kind of an overarching concept.

00:10:41.950 --> 00:10:46.390
 We definitely only mapped fruit that was growing in public space.

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:53.650
 Or if it was growing, if a tree was growing in somebody else's yard, then that branches well over a sidewalk.

00:10:54.230 --> 00:11:02.330
 So, and then we also, you know, think that you shouldn't pick fruit that's beyond the fence or within private property.

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:05.090
 Okay, that was my question.

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:08.010
 It's where the fence starts and stops that way.

00:11:08.250 --> 00:11:13.110
 And so, like, if someone really goes at it with their pruning, the next year you might be out of luck.

00:11:13.450 --> 00:11:15.050
 Yeah, matter of fact, that's happened before.

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:19.880
 There was this amazing avocado tree near Silver Lake.

00:11:20.140 --> 00:11:23.180
 And we used to go pick it, and we used to go pick it, and it was filled with avocados.

00:11:23.740 --> 00:11:27.020
 And then one day we came by, and the neighbor seemed really angry.

00:11:27.400 --> 00:11:30.030
 And so he just chopped the thing down.

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:32.300
 It's kind of sad.

00:11:32.540 --> 00:11:36.200
 It's really sad, but it just goes to show you how some people feel about sharing.

00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:43.040
 You know, it's like, that tree was actually huge and had, you know, hundreds of pounds of avocados on it a year.

00:11:43.460 --> 00:11:47.500
 And he could probably eat, you know, a small portion of that.

00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:55.210
 But some people love to share, and I think it's always good, too, you know, to ask.

00:11:56.680 --> 00:12:01.900
 And often, if you ask somebody who has a tree in their yard, they'll just say, yeah, pick it, please.

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:05.680
 Yeah, I mean, which is a common response.

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:09.960
 Like, what are you going to do with 400 pounds of avocados that all get ripe at the same time, right?

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:10.600
 It's a lot.

00:12:10.840 --> 00:12:11.220
 Yeah.

00:12:11.340 --> 00:12:12.040
 For one person.

00:12:13.770 --> 00:12:19.330
 So when I was preparing to talk to you guys, in one of the videos that you sent me,

00:12:19.330 --> 00:12:25.930
 I think, Austin, it was you that said, you know, a fruit tree is not more dangerous than any other tree,

00:12:26.680 --> 00:12:32.720
 which, like, made me laugh for, like, the rest of the day, like, about this idea that, like,

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:37.460
 there's all these dangerous trees all around, and especially, like, you know,

00:12:37.540 --> 00:12:41.080
 those plum trees in the middle of summer can get really slippery.

00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:41.860
 I know.

00:12:42.040 --> 00:12:42.280
 I know.

00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:48.240
 You can, I just imagine, like, a fruit doctor or something who goes around, you know,

00:12:48.860 --> 00:12:51.080
 helping people with fruit injuries.

00:12:52.270 --> 00:12:52.590
 Totally.

00:12:52.890 --> 00:12:53.150
 Yep.

00:12:53.310 --> 00:12:58.010
 Because there must be a lot of people with their eyes poked out and broken limbs.

00:12:59.220 --> 00:12:59.850
 Yeah.

00:12:59.950 --> 00:13:04.590
 I mean, speaking from experience, my stomach has hurt before when I've eaten too much fruit.

00:13:04.890 --> 00:13:06.450
 So that is dangerous.

00:13:06.690 --> 00:13:07.210
 It's true.

00:13:07.900 --> 00:13:10.710
 But what did you mean when you said that?

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:13.030
 Well, I think I literally meant that.

00:13:13.030 --> 00:13:22.380
 I mean, when we've planted fruit, and, you know, the idea of planting fruit trees in public space up until, like,

00:13:22.460 --> 00:13:25.060
 two or three years ago was just, like, no way.

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:25.860
 That's not happening.

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:31.900
 And basically the arguments were, you know, a child could get their eyes poked out.

00:13:32.620 --> 00:13:34.400
 They could climb and fall.

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:36.140
 They're going to throw that fruit at somebody.

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:38.100
 There's somebody who's going to slip on the fruit.

00:13:38.620 --> 00:13:40.660
 There's all these fears that come.

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:46.780
 There's all these reasons why people say that fruit can't be in the parkways in cities or in public parks.

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:57.670
 So when we planted a public fruit park in Delaware Park with the L.A. County Arts Commission in 2012,

00:13:58.270 --> 00:14:04.180
 it was a year long of meetings, like, trying to defend fruit trees.

00:14:04.630 --> 00:14:07.200
 So that's really where that statement came from.

00:14:07.240 --> 00:14:13.080
 It was, like, I feel like, you know, trying to stand up for a fruit tree all the time

00:14:13.080 --> 00:14:16.100
 and, like, a fruit tree's rights to exist in cities.

00:14:16.100 --> 00:14:21.780
 And it seems so strange because they're really beautiful and they have beautiful flowers

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:24.540
 and they taste good and they just keep on giving.

00:14:25.820 --> 00:14:32.900
 So now in Los Angeles, people are definitely more interested and excited about having fruit trees.

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:40.370
 And they've recently changed the laws in the parkways where people can now plant fruit trees with a permit.

00:14:41.350 --> 00:14:44.320
 So that sort of idea is changing slowly.

00:14:44.540 --> 00:14:49.820
 Okay, so you guys started, you were making maps, or you made a map of your neighborhood,

00:14:50.140 --> 00:14:51.780
 which was Silver Lake in Los Angeles.

00:14:52.370 --> 00:14:55.090
 And then what happened after that and how did that lead?

00:14:55.460 --> 00:14:59.700
 You were just talking about now there's a public fruit park that you guys have planted.

00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:06.980
 Well, I think quickly after the first map, realizing that there could be more,

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:14.100
 we had fantasies of making public fruit parks and transforming the city into, like, a communal garden

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:19.320
 or adding a new neighborhood resource where you could just not be hungry because you were there.

00:15:20.030 --> 00:15:24.890
 And in Southern California, you can do that so that a neighborhood is ever-bearing,

00:15:25.370 --> 00:15:28.070
 meaning every month of the year there could be something right.

00:15:28.270 --> 00:15:29.410
 That's not hard to organize.

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:36.960
 And journeying to get to make a fruit park took the good part of 10 years.

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:44.260
 It was a lot of work and a lot of different projects that built a vocabulary or a lexicon of work

00:15:44.260 --> 00:15:52.950
 that allowed, you know, the city and county to understand that this could be really a new idea for urban base.

00:15:53.770 --> 00:15:54.650
 It took a long time.

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:57.510
 We visited a lot of places and mapped a lot of neighborhoods.

00:15:57.510 --> 00:16:09.180
 Yeah, and also, you know, also, also, we have a lot of public artworks that fit into the realm of social practice.

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:15.640
 So we, so we, the, we were doing the public fruit jams and fruit tree adoptions

00:16:15.640 --> 00:16:23.750
 and all kinds of ways that we sort of activate people in this, around this idea of sharing fruit.

00:16:24.090 --> 00:16:29.270
 And now we've got new projects that we're doing, like Lemonade Stand,

00:16:29.630 --> 00:16:36.590
 where we invite people to have a glass of lemonade if they will draw a self-portrait on a lemon.

00:16:37.370 --> 00:16:42.970
 So it's like these kind of actions with the public that we're really excited about,

00:16:43.050 --> 00:16:46.290
 and we continue to do those as well as artworks.

00:16:46.790 --> 00:16:51.510
 So one thing I think about is when you guys, you were both talking about this a little bit,

00:16:51.510 --> 00:16:55.970
 but when you started in 2004, it was a pretty different world in terms of,

00:16:57.120 --> 00:17:03.000
 well, definitely with technology, but, but also in terms of like where the food movement was at, right?

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:04.080
 People were just starting.

00:17:04.580 --> 00:17:11.480
 It was, yeah, it was non-existent, you know, like the whole, it was, it was an, like, we were really,

00:17:11.800 --> 00:17:14.560
 we definitely were on the forefront of that whole thing.

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:27.210
 And I think that's really why Fallen Fruit also gained popularity as a, as an artwork, as a movement,

00:17:27.210 --> 00:17:35.740
 because we, we got, we would get interviews with NPR or different things, like just the idea was exciting to people.

00:17:35.740 --> 00:17:41.320
 I was going to say, you know, it goes back to the philosophy of Fallen Fruit that, you know,

00:17:41.320 --> 00:17:46.940
 we're really passionate about the idea of sharing, you know, an urban experience is authentic,

00:17:46.940 --> 00:17:52.600
 and everyone has one, and we share it, whether we're conscious of it or unconscious.

00:17:53.000 --> 00:18:00.230
 And then to consciously make a decision that you can create resources as a person that shares with other people,

00:18:00.230 --> 00:18:07.110
 people you may never know, to us, it's a radical idea, this sense of sharing at this profound level

00:18:07.110 --> 00:18:13.880
 that goes beyond you and your family and friends, that it goes into the future to people that you are guaranteed to never know.

00:18:14.070 --> 00:18:17.460
 To us, that fundamentally is part of the core messaging of Fallen Fruit.

00:18:18.020 --> 00:18:23.050
 Well, and so, I guess I'm kind of interested in, like, were you emerging artists at this time,

00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:29.100
 kind of growing into your careers, and how has that kind of followed along with being part of a food movement?

00:18:29.450 --> 00:18:34.030
 That's a great question. It's interesting. I don't know if we've ever exactly been at that.

00:18:35.330 --> 00:18:46.020
 I think I was, God, how, we started Fallen Fruit in 2004, so I have a pretty solid career as, I don't know,

00:18:46.080 --> 00:18:51.890
 a photographer and video artist up until then, but so, the thing about it.

00:18:51.890 --> 00:18:59.390
 Yeah, it was totally different for me. I was in graduate, so it was a pretty different world.

00:18:59.390 --> 00:19:04.590
 I would say, I would say, I don't even know if this is an interesting thing to talk about,

00:19:04.770 --> 00:19:10.690
 but I would say one of the most amazing things, Dave and I and Matthias, too,

00:19:11.250 --> 00:19:16.010
 you know, had always wanted to have a forum to express our art.

00:19:16.740 --> 00:19:23.380
 And it was interesting that such a simple idea that we all had together became so popular.

00:19:23.380 --> 00:19:31.260
 And then, you know, by 2010, we got a year-long show, exhibition, and curatorial project at LACMA.

00:19:31.860 --> 00:19:38.540
 I mean, and then we just had a series of big shows, and there couldn't be anything more exciting.

00:19:38.780 --> 00:19:50.440
 But the thing I have to say is that the intention behind it was so, it was and is so, I don't know, pure in a way.

00:19:50.860 --> 00:19:52.140
 I mean, that's what I would say.

00:19:52.500 --> 00:19:59.780
 Like, we really do love what we're doing, and we love the idea of planting fruit trees in public space.

00:19:59.780 --> 00:20:06.570
 So, I think that that makes sense to why the project would be successful.

00:20:06.830 --> 00:20:13.260
 But, yeah, I mean, as an artist, like, we are doing exactly what we always wanted to do.

00:20:13.850 --> 00:20:20.340
 And it felt, at the time, a little bit like, this is just cosmic luck, you know?

00:20:20.480 --> 00:20:23.460
 And at the same time, I would just say one more thing.

00:20:23.560 --> 00:20:28.340
 At the same time, the LA art scene was just starting to take off in such an amazing way.

00:20:28.980 --> 00:20:35.490
 And we were also part of that, like, Mark Allen machine project.

00:20:35.490 --> 00:20:38.970
 We were doing fruit jams with Mark Allen.

00:20:39.270 --> 00:20:42.770
 And so, it was just kind of like being in the right place at the right time.

00:20:43.390 --> 00:20:48.940
 I guess I asked that question because I'm interested in this idea of imaginative possibility.

00:20:49.400 --> 00:20:56.840
 And it seems like, for the two of you, fruit trees have this incredible imaginative possibility that just kind of keeps giving.

00:20:56.880 --> 00:21:01.640
 Yeah, I think the point that you're bringing up is really interesting for us to speak to.

00:21:01.640 --> 00:21:10.390
 One of the things that we ended up doing is having this profound shift of understanding after a few years,

00:21:10.450 --> 00:21:24.140
 where we realized that fruit and fruit trees and public space could actually be a material that we could apply all of these skills of art making from, you know,

00:21:24.300 --> 00:21:27.980
 I don't know, Austin and I, our whole lives, since we were teenagers, we were always artists.

00:21:27.980 --> 00:21:42.400
 So, we were able to take things that we learned in photography or other skills and performance and whatever and start thinking about it in a more, I don't know how to put it exactly,

00:21:42.560 --> 00:21:48.470
 but where instead of fruit being the subject of something or public space being the site, we started understanding,

00:21:48.650 --> 00:22:00.890
 oh my God, this could be like clay and collage and paper mache, that we could actually just completely sort of approach the idea of what we're doing in a really different way than a lot of other people might think.

00:22:00.890 --> 00:22:07.970
 Well, and that allowed us to really play with the public instead of the public, again, being an audience, we could think of it as being a collaboration.

00:22:07.970 --> 00:22:16.890
 Well, and that is exactly like what stands out to me about your work and this ongoing collaboration is using food as this place for a creative medium.

00:22:17.270 --> 00:22:21.120
 So, it's a little bit complicated to talk about, right?

00:22:21.180 --> 00:22:27.560
 Because I think that it somehow exists somewhere between this idea of fruit and public space and play.

00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:35.450
 Like, you guys are very playful in your work, but you're actually getting at some pretty serious things.

00:22:35.570 --> 00:22:43.290
 There was a moment when we realized that fruit is big on the planet, that everyone can relate to it.

00:22:44.240 --> 00:22:52.160
 And in its most primary sort of primal form, it's always the trees don't want anything back from you.

00:22:52.160 --> 00:23:00.840
 And in that way, we understood that the smallest child and their grandparents are both the experts of the taste of what a strawberry is.

00:23:01.020 --> 00:23:08.440
 Or, you know, we all eat the same bananas, regardless if we're rich or we're poor, or we come from one part of the world or another.

00:23:09.190 --> 00:23:10.850
 It's a unique material.

00:23:11.350 --> 00:23:21.280
 And that allowed us to really actually expand the idea of playing and not have to worry about being so academic in our approach or something like that.

00:23:22.120 --> 00:23:22.360
 Yeah.

00:23:22.760 --> 00:23:31.740
 That, to me, feels like a really different approach to, like, what an urban food system is than, I don't know, than I think than the way that city planners are approaching it.

00:23:31.740 --> 00:23:35.120
 Or even, you know, urban food activists.

00:23:35.300 --> 00:23:35.940
 Oh, yeah.

00:23:36.180 --> 00:23:38.580
 Yeah, I think they're, yeah, I totally agree with you.

00:23:38.720 --> 00:23:44.420
 So, it's actually amazing to, because we work with urban planners and food activists.

00:23:44.820 --> 00:23:48.710
 So, yeah, we're in a completely different space than that.

00:23:49.130 --> 00:24:00.050
 And it really, I think, does come down to the idea that Dave and I love to play when we're working.

00:24:00.530 --> 00:24:07.940
 We also have a clear understanding that our work is really profound, but that we present it in a really playful way.

00:24:08.620 --> 00:24:17.860
 And I think that it might be kind of obvious what we're hinting at in an artwork, but we kind of talk about it.

00:24:17.860 --> 00:24:20.680
 We'll talk about it in ways that just makes it look like fun.

00:24:20.680 --> 00:24:32.630
 And this is, it's a really conscious thing that we do in that we really want to, we really want to make our project for everyone and bring people together and have a good time.

00:24:32.630 --> 00:24:42.790
 But then, if, I don't know if it sounds cliche or what, but we do, then we just leave space for people to not even imagine that it's art.

00:24:43.880 --> 00:24:49.030
 But just, maybe they're just doing, you know, eating a watermelon, but...

00:24:49.850 --> 00:24:53.090
 I'm getting so excited about what Austin's saying.

00:24:53.390 --> 00:24:54.330
 I'm smiling.

00:24:54.740 --> 00:24:56.320
 And you can't see that.

00:24:57.820 --> 00:25:00.730
 I love that Austin says this.

00:25:00.730 --> 00:25:16.450
 And I think it's one of my personal favorite things about what we get to do at Fallen Fruit is that we're so concerned about leaving the space for their own theory and their own ideas and their own...

00:25:16.450 --> 00:25:19.230
 People don't even understand it's art.

00:25:19.420 --> 00:25:20.540
 And they don't need to.

00:25:20.660 --> 00:25:21.300
 Who cares?

00:25:22.120 --> 00:25:26.710
 And it's okay if the watermelon's a watermelon, but it also might be art at the same time.

00:25:26.790 --> 00:25:30.690
 It's really one of the greatest pleasures in working on Fallen Fruit Zone.

00:25:30.750 --> 00:25:32.790
 Sorry to interrupt you often.

00:25:33.050 --> 00:25:34.740
 I just, I love it so much.

00:25:34.820 --> 00:25:35.780
 I had to jump in.

00:25:35.780 --> 00:25:37.640
 Tell me how you do that.

00:25:37.800 --> 00:25:40.140
 Tell me how that's happened with these projects over time.

00:25:40.380 --> 00:25:41.920
 Like, what about with public fruit jams?

00:25:41.960 --> 00:25:47.020
 Well, the public fruit jam was interesting because I think that's how we learned how to collaborate with the public.

00:25:48.030 --> 00:25:55.200
 And it was a really important project for Fallen Fruit in our making, learning and making.

00:25:55.990 --> 00:26:00.710
 One of the things that was so important about the jam being successful is that nobody ever used recipes.

00:26:01.370 --> 00:26:04.060
 We didn't have recipes and we don't have ingredients.

00:26:04.710 --> 00:26:07.920
 We had materials and we had ratios and guidelines.

00:26:07.920 --> 00:26:12.780
 And the way people learned how to make jam was to hang out with somebody else they didn't know.

00:26:13.170 --> 00:26:22.220
 It wasn't really a process of learning how to be an expert jam maker or a master class or anything like that.

00:26:22.400 --> 00:26:28.960
 It was kind of a frenzy that was fueled by, you know, public fruit and fructose and a little bit of pectin.

00:26:28.960 --> 00:26:56.120
 Then it was amazing because what would happen when people would sit down together with people they don't know and start cutting fruit is that people would start talking about, you know, their family history and how maybe their grandmother used to store apples in her basement wrapped in paper or different recipes their family had or memories of fruit.

00:26:56.120 --> 00:27:03.200
 So, so we, so we kind of started to get this understanding how embedded fruit is in culture.

00:27:03.360 --> 00:27:04.170
 Right.

00:27:04.290 --> 00:27:09.830
 And in that you have all of these things and you have all these people together, but you're not really sure what the outcome is going to be.

00:27:10.030 --> 00:27:13.090
 It seems like you guys set up a lot of situations like that.

00:27:13.210 --> 00:27:13.450
 Yeah.

00:27:13.650 --> 00:27:19.250
 And then, then what the outcome would inevitably be would be like new friends were made.

00:27:19.470 --> 00:27:21.170
 People had a great time.

00:27:22.430 --> 00:27:23.990
 New relationships formed.

00:27:23.990 --> 00:27:37.790
 So we had really decided that the art is not the jam itself or the jars of jam, but the art was just the idea of bringing these people together and how they interacted.

00:27:38.090 --> 00:27:42.620
 Well, so let's talk about the public fruit park and how that came about.

00:27:42.760 --> 00:27:45.820
 You guys did a lot of work in so many places, right?

00:27:45.860 --> 00:27:55.380
 All over the world and you were making these maps and you were having these public fruit jams, but were you always kind of thinking about what would it be like to have this public fruit park?

00:27:55.380 --> 00:28:07.560
 Yeah, I mean, we, we wanted that so badly, pretty much at any opportunity that we were presented where we might flirt with the idea, we did it.

00:28:07.780 --> 00:28:22.730
 So, for example, the first attempt, the first expression of what could be a public fruit park, we were invited to do a project in Colonia Federal, which is at the border of the U.S. and Mexico and Tijuana.

00:28:22.730 --> 00:28:30.760
 And it's the, um, the neighborhood literally on the border fence and we were invited to do an art project.

00:28:30.880 --> 00:28:41.090
 So what we put forward was to plant fruit trees on the border in barrels could be moved because people don't live there very long.

00:28:41.190 --> 00:28:44.600
 They might stay for a few months and then they go across the border.

00:28:44.700 --> 00:28:47.880
 So it's very transient and it historically has always been that way.

00:28:47.880 --> 00:28:55.570
 So we put fruit trees in containers and gifted them to the neighborhood and then the residents could push them to their friend's house down the block when they leave.

00:28:55.590 --> 00:28:59.610
 So the trees could carry on in a way the neighborhood already behaved.

00:29:00.380 --> 00:29:01.140
 That's pretty amazing.

00:29:01.400 --> 00:29:03.700
 What, when ended up happening with those trees?

00:29:03.840 --> 00:29:10.500
 The trees are still really, yeah, they're doing really well and they're really, they're happy and growing as people have really taken care of them.

00:29:11.430 --> 00:29:11.810
 I bet.

00:29:11.990 --> 00:29:14.570
 That seems like such a nice way to call a place home.

00:29:15.840 --> 00:29:17.420
 So, so that was the first iteration.

00:29:17.420 --> 00:29:20.940
 And then how did this come about in L.A.?

00:29:20.940 --> 00:29:42.430
 Let's see, what, it's the L.A. County Arts Commission invited us to apply to do a piece at Delaware Park and, and so awarded the project and they, they helped guide us through the whole, the whole process, which really, a lot of it was going to several meetings with different, different parts of city government.

00:29:42.750 --> 00:29:45.870
 So it was a, it was, that was a really great experience.

00:29:46.150 --> 00:29:49.300
 That was our first, first big park with the city.

00:29:49.630 --> 00:29:51.220
 Are there more?

00:29:51.380 --> 00:29:51.620
 Oh yeah.

00:29:51.940 --> 00:30:02.390
 Since Del Air Park in 2012, we, um, we installed a trailhead at, or Park to Playa at, um, Stalker and La Brea.

00:30:03.120 --> 00:30:07.770
 Um, well, there's about 40 fruit trees in the park.

00:30:07.770 --> 00:30:27.300
 Yeah, we have, we have, um, opening, I think in November of this year is Stoneview Nature Center, which we, we were the artists awarded to design the artwork and, and the park with the landscape architect and the architect.

00:30:27.300 --> 00:30:33.270
 So there's, we've created a series of fruit tree installations in the park.

00:30:33.470 --> 00:30:34.630
 It's pretty, it's pretty awesome.

00:30:34.630 --> 00:30:39.150
 And that's going to be fun to, when that opens, uh, that's another piece.

00:30:39.250 --> 00:30:42.750
 And then we're, we're working on our endless orchard project.

00:30:42.890 --> 00:30:53.810
 So leading up to that, we were able to plant fruit trees in Lafayette park and MacArthur park, uh, with hearts of Los Angeles.

00:30:53.810 --> 00:30:56.950
 This is this great school program in Westlake district.

00:30:56.950 --> 00:31:07.390
 And then we, we, we've, we've put a bunch of fruit trees in barrels and, and on the margins of public space around the Los Angeles state historic park.

00:31:07.390 --> 00:31:08.310
 That's going to be opening.

00:31:08.770 --> 00:31:10.720
 Um, and we did that with community.

00:31:10.720 --> 00:31:23.890
 We actually went, we actually went door to door and got people to agree to take care of fruit trees, plant them, water them, and share them with anybody who passes by.

00:31:24.090 --> 00:31:33.810
 So, I don't know, we've probably planted about a 150 fruit trees around, around that area in Chinatown, downtown LA.

00:31:34.640 --> 00:31:37.190
 And how do you decide what trees to plant?

00:31:37.470 --> 00:31:39.010
 You mean varieties or?

00:31:39.170 --> 00:31:39.370
 Yeah.

00:31:39.490 --> 00:31:41.930
 Well, I mean, we don't just work in Los Angeles.

00:31:42.130 --> 00:31:46.790
 We work in a lot of different geographies, but we always want to pick trees that are going to do well.

00:31:47.030 --> 00:31:49.670
 So, we think about a variety of things.

00:31:51.460 --> 00:32:02.510
 Quality of fruit is important to us, so we don't want to pick things that are going to not be as sweet as they could be, or maybe a drought tolerant for Southern California, et cetera.

00:32:03.750 --> 00:32:12.850
 One of the things that people don't realize that we spend a lot of energy on is making sure that there's a really big variety so that things all don't get ripe at the same time.

00:32:13.100 --> 00:32:14.320
 I mean, that's not interesting.

00:32:14.440 --> 00:32:15.340
 That's an orchard.

00:32:15.480 --> 00:32:17.440
 That's a commercial crop.

00:32:18.030 --> 00:32:20.550
 What we're interested in is changing your experience.

00:32:22.330 --> 00:32:27.470
 So, we like to make sure that fruit trees are ripe as much of the year as possible in a neighborhood.

00:32:27.980 --> 00:32:31.720
 Right now, we've also gotten really excited about drought tolerant fruit.

00:32:32.140 --> 00:32:37.350
 So, we've been looking at propagating dragon fruit and tunas.

00:32:37.550 --> 00:32:43.090
 We're thinking about maybe planting pineapple around, which does really well in this climate.

00:32:44.260 --> 00:32:45.740
 They're small, but they grow.

00:32:47.120 --> 00:32:53.990
 So, we're kind of like thinking about how can we plant fruit trees that maybe don't need as much water and care.

00:32:53.990 --> 00:33:05.200
 Yeah, pomegranates, and of course, we're working with these more water, how do you describe that?

00:33:06.620 --> 00:33:07.600
 Embracing, yeah.

00:33:08.900 --> 00:33:12.400
 Yeah, I don't know, drought friendly fruit, something.

00:33:13.020 --> 00:33:14.380
 Yeah, yeah, something like that.

00:33:14.480 --> 00:33:18.330
 Like, things that just aren't really hard on our infrastructure.

00:33:18.730 --> 00:33:20.610
 We're really exploring that right now.

00:33:20.890 --> 00:33:24.410
 Yeah, we're loving the idea of desert fruits.

00:33:24.410 --> 00:33:45.260
 Well, so, my grandpa was raised in Long Beach, and he grew up during the Depression, and he famously told this story of crawling through the orange orchards on his belly, like picking up oranges with his brothers so they would have enough food to eat.

00:33:46.430 --> 00:33:52.070
 And, you know, when I think about LA, I think about LA is this amazingly diverse place, right?

00:33:52.170 --> 00:33:54.230
 It's got people from all over the world.

00:33:54.950 --> 00:34:12.370
 And I think, I don't know, because it's such a car city, like we were talking about at the beginning of this interview, I don't have this great imagination of, like, people's backyards or these public places where people cultivate food, and especially fruit trees from all the places they've come in the world.

00:34:12.370 --> 00:34:16.690
 But, you know, like, through hearing you guys, obviously they do.

00:34:17.010 --> 00:34:24.590
 And how do these trees and how do these varieties that you pick and how do these fruits that grow relate to these neighborhoods that they're in?

00:34:25.950 --> 00:34:29.460
 Well, Silver Lake is just filled with fruit.

00:34:29.620 --> 00:34:33.480
 There's different cultures who tend to like different fruits.

00:34:33.480 --> 00:34:37.480
 Like, what is it, the Calamondan limes?

00:34:38.360 --> 00:34:39.320
 Filipino lime.

00:34:39.620 --> 00:34:43.520
 The common name is Filipino lime or Calamondi or Calamondan.

00:34:43.700 --> 00:34:44.260
 Sorry to interrupt.

00:34:44.640 --> 00:34:44.880
 Yeah.

00:34:45.380 --> 00:34:47.580
 Or, like, there's tons of loquats around.

00:34:47.720 --> 00:34:49.420
 Like, there's tons of loquats.

00:34:50.120 --> 00:34:56.540
 And I can imagine, I don't know, I mean, who, I don't know who's into loquats.

00:34:56.620 --> 00:34:57.900
 Like, is it Chinese, Korean?

00:34:59.620 --> 00:35:04.600
 There's a lot of lemons, oranges, mandarins, avocados.

00:35:06.050 --> 00:35:17.940
 So, I think it's interesting when you go around, like, a neighborhood like Beverly Hills, you really don't find much fruit on the edge of people's property.

00:35:18.670 --> 00:35:24.020
 It seems to be these more diverse neighborhoods that have a lot of fruit.

00:35:24.020 --> 00:35:31.230
 Well, it's also really interesting as, like, a social study is that you can see the history of a neighborhood by fruit trees.

00:35:31.720 --> 00:35:37.230
 Even in cities away from Los Angeles, long ago, we were doing a project in Austria.

00:35:37.870 --> 00:35:45.780
 And after World War II, a neighborhood became very, it was required that it became very self-sufficient.

00:35:46.350 --> 00:35:49.020
 And so, we mapped it because there were so many fruit trees.

00:35:49.020 --> 00:35:55.060
 And, of course, in Austria, there are apple trees and quinn and maybe some grapes and things like this.

00:35:55.140 --> 00:36:01.260
 But there were so many trees that were planted by people who lived there that existed in public space.

00:36:01.320 --> 00:36:02.400
 It was really quite remarkable.

00:36:03.160 --> 00:36:06.530
 And so, it's an economic history, an cultural history.

00:36:06.830 --> 00:36:10.350
 And sometimes the varieties of trees really reflect that.

00:36:11.830 --> 00:36:12.050
 Yeah.

00:36:13.360 --> 00:36:17.030
 I definitely think about that in neighborhoods that I've lived in.

00:36:17.030 --> 00:36:21.170
 Yeah, and then there's actually, it's really exciting in L.A. because you've got history.

00:36:22.090 --> 00:36:28.910
 Like, downtown L.A., there were all grapevines in certain areas.

00:36:29.170 --> 00:36:32.090
 And some areas, it was orange groves and lemon groves.

00:36:32.270 --> 00:36:35.370
 And so, there's still remnants of some of these things.

00:36:36.230 --> 00:36:43.760
 And we sort of like the idea of putting things back where they might have been as well.

00:36:43.760 --> 00:36:49.060
 And then, there was a grape that, there's a grape, there's one grape that's native to the area.

00:36:49.740 --> 00:36:51.900
 Do you remember the name of the variety, Dave?

00:36:52.270 --> 00:36:54.380
 No, I can't remember the name off the top of my head.

00:36:54.420 --> 00:36:54.760
 I'm sorry.

00:36:55.850 --> 00:36:58.550
 So, that used to grow around the L.A. River.

00:36:59.250 --> 00:36:59.490
 I don't know.

00:36:59.530 --> 00:37:03.430
 It'd be fun to think about putting some of those grapes back along the river.

00:37:04.050 --> 00:37:04.640
 Totally.

00:37:04.800 --> 00:37:07.880
 To kind of trace these cultural histories of these fruits, right?

00:37:07.880 --> 00:37:08.120
 Yeah.

00:37:08.350 --> 00:37:10.130
 So, what's next for you guys?

00:37:11.480 --> 00:37:27.960
 We've been working on our Endless Orchard app, which is going to be a way that anybody anywhere in the world could plant a fruit tree in front of their property or in public space and map it and share it.

00:37:28.250 --> 00:37:28.660
 Yeah.

00:37:28.820 --> 00:37:33.830
 We're really thinking of it as being kind of the biggest collaborative project we've ever thought of.

00:37:33.830 --> 00:37:41.010
 So, anyone can help make the project bigger and change it in their own way and transform their own neighborhood experiences.

00:37:41.690 --> 00:37:42.400
 Cool.

00:37:42.520 --> 00:37:43.760
 When's it going to be?

00:37:44.100 --> 00:37:45.040
 When are you launching it?

00:37:45.200 --> 00:37:47.200
 We're going to have a beta version pretty soon.

00:37:47.460 --> 00:37:49.980
 So, I think within a month.

00:37:50.180 --> 00:37:57.530
 And then we're going to do some kind of celebration in downtown L.A. to officially launch it.

00:37:58.760 --> 00:38:02.720
 That's why we've been planting fruit trees around L.A.'s State Historic Park.

00:38:03.080 --> 00:38:06.100
 And we're actually going to make a piece inside the park.

00:38:06.360 --> 00:38:09.640
 And we're going to call that the center of the Endless Orchard.

00:38:10.990 --> 00:38:15.920
 So, we actually got awarded a creative capital for this project.

00:38:16.320 --> 00:38:18.100
 And we've gotten a lot of support from the public.

00:38:18.260 --> 00:38:19.160
 We did a big Kickstarter.

00:38:19.520 --> 00:38:21.200
 And I've gotten some donations.

00:38:22.700 --> 00:38:24.300
 It's been a lot of fun.

00:38:24.420 --> 00:38:34.340
 So, we've been planting, reaching out to different communities, planting barrels with fruit trees, planting fruit trees in the ground.

00:38:34.340 --> 00:38:41.220
 It's been sort of our focus for the last year really strongly.

00:38:41.740 --> 00:38:43.340
 It sounds like a great project.

00:38:43.500 --> 00:38:46.980
 You'll have to make sure to send me the links so we can include that with the show.

00:38:47.340 --> 00:38:48.310
 Thanks, guys.

00:38:48.600 --> 00:38:49.900
 It was so nice to talk to you.

00:38:50.140 --> 00:38:50.780
 Thanks, Chelsea.

00:38:51.020 --> 00:38:52.340
 It was great talking to you.

00:38:52.880 --> 00:38:54.500
 You asked the best questions.

00:38:54.620 --> 00:38:55.020
 Thank you.

